Start With a Clear Picture of Your Goals
Before contacting any agency, take a step back and define what success actually looks like for your business. Are you trying to generate more phone calls? Increase foot traffic to a physical location? Build an email list? Improve visibility in local search results?
Different agencies tend to specialize in different areas, even when their websites list a full menu of services. Some are built around SEO and content strategy, others focus heavily on paid advertising, and some center their work on social media and brand design. Knowing your priorities ahead of time makes it much easier to tell whether a particular agency's strengths actually match what your business needs.

Questions Worth Asking Before You Commit
A capable agency should be comfortable answering direct questions rather than redirecting the conversation back to a sales pitch. A few worth bringing up include:
    • Who specifically will be managing my account? Some agencies hand your business off to junior staff once the contract is signed. 
    • How will you measure results? Be cautious of answers focused only on impressions or followers without any connection to leads or revenue. 
    • Can you share examples of past client work? Even anonymized case studies give you a sense of real outcomes rather than hypothetical promises. 
    • What does reporting look like, and how often? A monthly PDF with no explanation isn't the same as a team that walks you through what's actually working. 
    • What are the contract terms if I want to cancel? Agreement lengths and exit terms vary widely between agencies. [ADD CONTRACT TERMS] 

Warning Signs to Watch For
Not every agency operates the same way, and a few patterns are worth watching closely. Vague reporting that highlights vanity metrics, like impressions, without tying them to business outcomes is one common issue. Promises of guaranteed rankings or guaranteed follower counts are another red flag, since no legitimate agency can honestly make that kind of guarantee.
Long-term contracts without a clear cancellation path deserve extra scrutiny, as does poor communication after the agreement is signed. If an agency becomes difficult to reach once payment starts, that pattern tends to continue rather than improve.

